%erg 0.1
Title: The acceptance arena runs as a dedicated user, never as the operator
Created: 2026-09-03
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-03T12:51Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

Ruled by the author 2026-09-03 (`DECISIONS.md`): *"The alt user is not
optional."* The acceptance harness must run its arena as a dedicated account,
never as the operator, and this binds every assertion rather than only the
uninstall clause that provoked it.

The harness installs software it does not control, runs it against a library and
sweeps a filesystem afterwards — all of it, so far, under the operator's own
identity, where a target can read his mail, his keys and his research and a
harness bug can delete them. `sandbox.py`'s namespace is not a substitute: it is
bound to the R10 egress arm, the residue and durability assertions run outside
it, and it is probed rather than guaranteed, being unusable rootless on the
second machine. The account is the floor and the namespace is the ceiling.

## Relevant files

- `bench/acceptance/adapters/*.py` — every adapter starts a target process; that
  spawn is what has to cross a user boundary.
- `bench/acceptance/interface.py` — `Declaration.process`, which is where an
  adapter says how its target starts and stops.
- `bench/acceptance/sandbox.py` — the namespace mechanism, which composes with
  this and does not replace it.
- `Makefile` — `ACCEPTANCE_ARENA`, today `$(HOME)/data/acceptance-arena`.
- `bench/acceptance/run.py` — `--arena`, and where a missing account must turn
  into `not-run` rather than a fallback.

## Actions

1. Decide the mechanism before writing any of it, because it sets everything
   else: the harness re-executing itself as the account, versus each adapter
   spawning its target under it. The first gives one boundary and one place to
   get wrong; the second leaves the harness's own file writes as the operator,
   which is where the artifacts have to land anyway. Argue it; do not pick by
   convenience.
2. Provision the account reproducibly, as a script in the repository rather than
   a step in someone's memory, and on both machines. Name what privilege the
   provisioning needs and what the run itself needs, which should be less.
3. Make the arena and its contents belong to that account, and say in the
   artifact which account owned a run — a preserved arena an operator can no
   longer read is a surprise worth documenting rather than discovering.
4. Make absence honest: no account, no run. `run.py` reports `not-run` with the
   reason, and never falls back to the operator.

## Test

The control is the one that matters and it is cheap: a fixture target that writes
to a path owned by the operator and outside the arena must **fail to write it**
under the new posture, where today it would succeed. Run it against the current
tree first to see it succeed — that is the red — then under the account.

A second, weaker check: the artifact records the running account, and a run
started as the operator is refused rather than recorded.

## Verification

- [ ] a target process cannot write to an operator-owned path outside the arena,
      demonstrated by a fixture that succeeds before the change
- [ ] a machine without the account reports `not-run`, with the reason, for every
      assertion that needs one
- [ ] the artifact names the account a run belonged to

## Invariants

- No fallback to the operator, ever, for any reason including "the account is
  missing" and "this is only a fixture run".
- The namespace mechanism is not weakened or made conditional on this: they
  compose, and R10's egress arm keeps its own isolation.
- Existing artifacts are not rewritten. A measurement already taken stands as
  what it measured; carrying it forward is a re-run, not a re-reading.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] every assertion runs under the dedicated account on both machines, or
      reports `not-run` where it does not exist
- [ ] the uninstall clause's other preconditions — `TMPDIR` and `XDG_*`
      redirection — are satisfied or explicitly still open
- [ ] `make check` green; `./tickets/erg check tickets/` green
